WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.296
Welcome!

00:00:01.296 --> 00:00:04.365
In this Java tutorial, you'll use interfaces,

00:00:04.365 --> 00:00:06.888
which describe shared operations, and

00:00:06.888 --> 00:00:09.753
polymorphism, which lets different objects

00:00:09.753 --> 00:00:12.549
answer the same request in their own way.

00:00:13.254 --> 00:00:16.075
These ideas help applications work with

00:00:16.075 --> 00:00:19.402
different kinds of records or services through

00:00:19.402 --> 00:00:22.078
one shared operation, so adding a new

00:00:22.078 --> 00:00:25.043
implementation doesn't require a separate

00:00:25.043 --> 00:00:26.779
version of every caller.

00:00:27.468 --> 00:00:30.559
Today we'll help campus staff announce a room

00:00:30.559 --> 00:00:32.826
location and a course identifier.

00:00:32.826 --> 00:00:35.298
Each sign will supply its own label.

00:00:35.298 --> 00:00:38.389
Both announcements must start with Ready, and

00:00:38.389 --> 00:00:41.068
the room must appear before the course.

00:00:41.715 --> 00:00:44.824
We'll describe the shared request, implement two

00:00:44.824 --> 00:00:47.675
kinds of signs, and use one loop to announce

00:00:47.675 --> 00:00:47.998
them.

00:00:47.998 --> 00:00:50.590
A default method will provide the common

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:51.302
formatting.

00:00:51.302 --> 00:00:54.347
First we'll trace a worked example; then you'll

00:00:54.347 --> 00:00:56.290
predict a fresh pair of signs.

00:00:56.348 --> 00:00:59.248
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:00:59.248 --> 00:00:59.923
Main.java.

00:01:03.699 --> 00:01:04.939
The file is open.

00:01:04.939 --> 00:01:08.296
Our caller needs a text label from either kind

00:01:08.296 --> 00:01:11.579
of sign, without knowing how that sign stores

00:01:11.579 --> 00:01:12.747
its information.

00:01:12.747 --> 00:01:16.249
Let's give that shared request a name: Labelled.

00:01:20.153 --> 00:01:23.000
The interface keyword introduces our shared

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:23.331
type.

00:01:23.331 --> 00:01:26.509
Label is a required method that returns a String

00:01:26.509 --> 00:01:28.032
and takes no arguments.

00:01:28.032 --> 00:01:31.012
Its declaration ends with a semicolon because

00:01:31.012 --> 00:01:34.057
the implementing classes will supply the body.

00:01:34.057 --> 00:01:37.037
This required interface method is public even

00:01:37.037 --> 00:01:39.553
though we haven't written public here.

00:01:39.562 --> 00:01:42.787
Every announcement also needs the same prefix.

00:01:42.787 --> 00:01:45.731
Rather than repeat that formatting in both

00:01:45.731 --> 00:01:49.095
classes, let's put a default announcement method

00:01:49.095 --> 00:01:50.287
in the interface.

00:01:56.563 --> 00:01:59.552
A default method has a body that implementing

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:00.881
classes can inherit.

00:02:00.881 --> 00:02:03.936
Announcement asks the receiving object for its

00:02:03.936 --> 00:02:05.995
label and adds Ready before it.

00:02:05.995 --> 00:02:08.984
The formatting is shared, but the label still

00:02:08.984 --> 00:02:11.176
comes from the particular object.

00:02:11.176 --> 00:02:14.232
We now need a class that can answer that label

00:02:14.232 --> 00:02:14.763
request.

00:02:14.774 --> 00:02:18.057
Our first sign will remember a room location.

00:02:18.057 --> 00:02:21.414
Let's define RoomLabel as an implementation of

00:02:21.414 --> 00:02:24.332
Labelled, keep its location private, and

00:02:24.332 --> 00:02:27.324
initialize that field in the constructor.

00:02:37.291 --> 00:02:39.697
Implements establishes the interface

00:02:39.697 --> 00:02:40.566
relationship.

00:02:40.566 --> 00:02:43.574
The constructor copies its room argument into

00:02:43.574 --> 00:02:46.649
this object's private field, using the pattern

00:02:46.649 --> 00:02:48.320
from our earlier classes.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:51.528
That gives the object a location to remember; we

00:02:51.528 --> 00:02:54.403
still need the public method that turns the

00:02:54.403 --> 00:02:56.341
location into a useful label.

00:02:56.356 --> 00:02:59.691
Let's supply the required label method and mark

00:02:59.691 --> 00:03:03.026
it with the Override annotation so the compiler

00:03:03.026 --> 00:03:05.581
can check the intended relationship.

00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:15.413
This method returns Room followed by the stored

00:03:15.413 --> 00:03:16.016
location.

00:03:16.016 --> 00:03:18.894
Public preserves the access promised by the

00:03:18.894 --> 00:03:19.564
interface.

00:03:19.564 --> 00:03:22.777
Override is an annotation, not a method call: it

00:03:22.777 --> 00:03:25.723
helps catch a mismatched method name, but it

00:03:25.723 --> 00:03:28.467
doesn't create the implementation for us.

00:03:28.467 --> 00:03:31.346
A room named West will therefore supply the

00:03:31.346 --> 00:03:32.417
label Room West.

00:03:32.431 --> 00:03:35.460
A course sign needs the same operation but

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:36.542
different data.

00:03:36.542 --> 00:03:39.788
Let's give CourseLabel its own private course

00:03:39.788 --> 00:03:43.106
identifier and initialize it in a constructor.

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:55.807
The course object now remembers its own

00:03:55.807 --> 00:03:56.540
identifier.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:59.470
Its field has a different name from the room

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:02.467
field, and the caller won't need either name.

00:04:02.467 --> 00:04:05.598
What connects the two classes is their promised

00:04:05.598 --> 00:04:06.663
label operation.

00:04:06.663 --> 00:04:09.394
Let's complete the course version of that

00:04:09.394 --> 00:04:09.860
method.

00:04:16.863 --> 00:04:19.526
CourseLabel returns Course followed by its

00:04:19.526 --> 00:04:20.668
stored identifier.

00:04:20.668 --> 00:04:23.521
Both classes now answer the same request with

00:04:23.521 --> 00:04:25.043
different method bodies.

00:04:25.043 --> 00:04:28.087
Neither class replaces announcement, so both can

00:04:28.087 --> 00:04:29.799
use the default formatting.

00:04:29.799 --> 00:04:32.716
The interface checks the operation's shape; we

00:04:32.716 --> 00:04:35.697
still need to test that each returned label has

00:04:35.697 --> 00:04:36.838
the right meaning.

00:04:36.862 --> 00:04:39.602
Let's bring the signs together in Main.

00:04:39.602 --> 00:04:42.974
Main is the class containing our caller, and its

00:04:42.974 --> 00:04:45.854
main method is where this program starts.

00:04:45.854 --> 00:04:49.015
We'll construct a West room sign and a JAVA-1

00:04:49.015 --> 00:04:50.912
course sign, in that order.

00:05:04.841 --> 00:05:07.727
The array's element type is Labelled, so each

00:05:07.727 --> 00:05:10.741
element can hold a reference to an implementing

00:05:10.741 --> 00:05:11.190
object.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:13.498
The objects remain a RoomLabel and a

00:05:13.498 --> 00:05:16.127
CourseLabel; using an interface reference

00:05:16.127 --> 00:05:18.821
doesn't change their classes or copy their

00:05:18.821 --> 00:05:19.270
fields.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:21.706
This array fixes the room-first order.

00:05:21.706 --> 00:05:24.592
We can now request one announcement from each

00:05:24.592 --> 00:05:25.041
object.

00:05:34.292 --> 00:05:37.483
On each iteration, sign holds the next reference

00:05:37.483 --> 00:05:39.811
and has the declared type Labelled.

00:05:39.811 --> 00:05:42.869
Its announcement call enters the default body,

00:05:42.869 --> 00:05:45.396
which calls label on that same object.

00:05:45.396 --> 00:05:48.255
The actual object's class selects the label

00:05:48.255 --> 00:05:49.252
implementation.

00:05:49.252 --> 00:05:52.311
Let's click Run and follow those calls through

00:05:52.311 --> 00:05:53.042
the output.

00:05:58.104 --> 00:06:00.320
The first line is Ready: Room West.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:03.106
The loop visits the room object, whose label

00:06:03.106 --> 00:06:05.955
method supplies Room West; the default method

00:06:05.955 --> 00:06:06.651
adds Ready.

00:06:06.651 --> 00:06:09.184
The second line is Ready: Course JAVA-1.

00:06:09.184 --> 00:06:12.159
Now the same default body reaches CourseLabel's

00:06:12.159 --> 00:06:12.982
label method.

00:06:12.982 --> 00:06:15.958
The loop and declared reference type stayed the

00:06:15.958 --> 00:06:18.554
same, while the receiving object changed.

00:06:19.242 --> 00:06:22.655
The worked announcements follow the requested

00:06:22.655 --> 00:06:25.158
order and share their formatting.

00:06:25.158 --> 00:06:28.646
Let's test a fresh pair: room Lab A and course

00:06:28.646 --> 00:06:29.253
CSC-239.

00:06:29.253 --> 00:06:32.893
We'll keep the same definitions so you can trace

00:06:32.893 --> 00:06:35.017
the mechanism with new data.

00:06:59.879 --> 00:07:03.185
The interface and room implementation are back

00:07:03.185 --> 00:07:03.831
in place.

00:07:03.831 --> 00:07:07.209
Notice that the default method still requests a

00:07:07.209 --> 00:07:10.586
label from its receiving object; it contains no

00:07:10.586 --> 00:07:11.736
fixed room name.

00:07:11.736 --> 00:07:14.754
Let's add the course implementation again.

00:07:30.071 --> 00:07:32.155
Both implementations are ready.

00:07:32.155 --> 00:07:35.045
Let's construct the new signs in room-first

00:07:35.045 --> 00:07:37.801
order and use the same announcement loop.

00:07:37.801 --> 00:07:41.027
The new constructor arguments should provide the

00:07:41.027 --> 00:07:43.850
data; the method bodies should not need to

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:44.321
change.

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:08.961
Before running, predict the two complete output

00:08:08.961 --> 00:08:09.348
lines.

00:08:09.348 --> 00:08:12.250
For each iteration, name the actual receiving

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:15.087
class and the label method called inside the

00:08:15.087 --> 00:08:16.893
default announcement method.

00:08:16.893 --> 00:08:19.859
Explain which part determines the output order

00:08:19.859 --> 00:08:22.052
and which part supplies the label.

00:08:22.052 --> 00:08:24.180
Pause here if you need more time.

00:08:32.282 --> 00:08:34.507
Let's click Run and compare the two

00:08:34.507 --> 00:08:36.732
announcements with your prediction.

00:08:41.886 --> 00:08:45.181
The first announcement is Ready: Room Lab A, and

00:08:45.181 --> 00:08:47.652
the second is Ready: Course CSC-239.

00:08:47.652 --> 00:08:50.810
The array puts RoomLabel first and CourseLabel

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:51.291
second.

00:08:51.291 --> 00:08:54.243
Each object supplies its own label, and the

00:08:54.243 --> 00:08:57.538
inherited default method adds the common prefix.

00:08:57.538 --> 00:09:00.627
Changing the input text required no change to

00:09:00.627 --> 00:09:02.961
either implementation or the loop.

00:09:03.703 --> 00:09:06.788
We built one caller that announces two different

00:09:06.788 --> 00:09:07.752
kinds of signs.

00:09:07.752 --> 00:09:10.708
The interface describes the shared operations,

00:09:10.708 --> 00:09:13.664
polymorphic calls reach the receiving object's

00:09:13.664 --> 00:09:16.748
implementation, and the default method keeps the

00:09:16.748 --> 00:09:18.483
common formatting together.

00:09:18.483 --> 00:09:21.568
The output confirms both the requested order and

00:09:21.568 --> 00:09:23.303
the object-specific labels.

00:09:24.042 --> 00:09:26.768
Suppose staff add an event sign to the same

00:09:26.768 --> 00:09:27.149
array.

00:09:27.149 --> 00:09:29.875
What operation must its class provide, what

00:09:29.875 --> 00:09:32.729
should that operation mean, and why could the

00:09:32.729 --> 00:09:35.709
existing loop announce it without a new branch?

00:09:35.709 --> 00:09:38.499
Describe one test that would check the event

00:09:38.499 --> 00:09:41.225
label's meaning, not just its String result

00:09:41.225 --> 00:09:41.542
type.
